@page "/docs/get-started/ai-assistants"

@* Connecting an AI assistant to IzzyBlazor: izzy agent (the skill and both servers, src/IzzyBlazor.Cli/AgentFiles.cs),
   izzy mcp's tools (BuildTools.cs), the docs MCP server at /mcp (Services/Mcp), llms.txt and the API metadata.
   The client settings were the documented ones for each client in Oct 2026; the skill folders in Nov 2026. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="ai-assistants">
    <Lead>
        An AI assistant guesses at a library it hasn't seen, and IzzyBlazor is newer than its training data. When you
        connect your assistant to IzzyBlazor's MCP server, it looks up components, parameters and working examples for the
        release this site documents instead.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Set up your project</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy agent</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Run it in your app's folder. It needs the <Anchor Href="docs/cli/overview">izzy CLI</Anchor>. It sets up three things:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                A skill that teaches your assistant to build with IzzyBlazor: look a component up first, keep pages
                static where they can be, and use a block before writing a section by hand. It goes in
                <Code>.claude/skills</Code> and <Code>.agents/skills</Code>, which Claude Code, Copilot, Cursor, Codex,
                Gemini CLI, Junie and Windsurf read.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>The docs server, which looks up components, parameters and examples.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The izzy server, which adds blocks and pages to your app.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Both servers go in <Code>.mcp.json</Code>, <Code>.vscode/mcp.json</Code> and <Code>.cursor/mcp.json</Code>.
            If you already have one of these files, izzy keeps your servers and adds its two. If the file has
            comments, izzy leaves it alone and prints the lines to add. Run <Code>izzy agent</Code> again after you
            update izzy: it replaces the skill with the new one. To see what it would write, add
            <Code>--dry-run</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The izzy server</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy mcp</Code> runs izzy's commands as tools on your machine, so your assistant gets the same result
            you get in a terminal. Every tool needs a free account: run <Code>izzy login</Code> once. The tools list
            Pro items too, and add them when you have Pro.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="BuildTools" Caption="The izzy server's tools" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Tool" Width="Length.Rem(10)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Answers</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The tools that write take <Code>dryRun</Code>, which answers with the changes and writes nothing. They don't
            replace a file you've changed unless the assistant sets <Code>overwrite</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To add the server by hand in another client, add a stdio server that runs <Code>izzy mcp</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The docs server</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The server's URL is <Code>@Endpoint</Code>. It uses the Streamable HTTP transport and doesn't need a login or
            key. Its tools read the docs, except <Code>report_gap</Code>, which posts a public report when you agree
            to it. Its answers cover the release this site documents.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Claude Code</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">claude mcp add --transport http izzyblazor @Endpoint</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To share the server with everyone on the project, add <Code>--scope project</Code>. Claude Code then saves it in
            the repository's <Code>.mcp.json</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">VS Code and Visual Studio</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In VS Code, add the server to <Code>.vscode/mcp.json</Code>. In Visual Studio, add it to <Code>.mcp.json</Code>
            next to the solution. Then use it from Copilot's agent mode:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="json" Title=".vscode/mcp.json">@VsCodeJson</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Cursor</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="json" Title=".cursor/mcp.json">@CursorJson</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Claude apps</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In Claude's desktop app or on claude.ai, open Settings, then Connectors, add a custom connector and paste
            <Code>@Endpoint</Code>. Any other client that supports Streamable HTTP uses the same URL.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The docs server's tools</Heading>
        <Table Items="Tools" Caption="The docs server's tools" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Tool" Width="Length.Rem(10)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Answers with">@context!.Answers</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The tools answer in Markdown, with links to this site. Describe what you're building, such as "a settings form
            that posts on static pages", and the assistant picks the tools. To help it use them, name IzzyBlazor in your
            request.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Without MCP</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <Anchor Href="llms.txt">llms.txt</Anchor>: describes IzzyBlazor and links to every article and component, for
                assistants that read websites.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <Anchor Href="izzyblazor.api.json">API metadata</Anchor>: every component's parameters, events, methods and
                types in one JSON file, for your own tools.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/create-app">Create an app</Anchor>: <Code>--mcp</Code> connects a new app to the docs server.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what components do on static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Endpoint = "https://izzyblazor.com/mcp";

    private sealed record Tool(string Name, string Answers);

    private static readonly Tool[] Tools =
    [
        new("search", "Components, parameters, types, examples and articles that match the search words"),
        new("list_components", "Every component by category, with a summary and any render mode limits"),
        new("get_component", "A component's API: parameters with types and defaults, events, methods, render mode support, parts, example ids and accessibility"),
        new("get_type", "An enum's values, a type's members, or the shared style props"),
        new("get_example", "An example's whole .razor source"),
        new("get_article", "An article or a component's Overview, with its examples' source"),
        new("find_known_issues", "Troubleshooting articles, render mode limits and open GitHub issues about a problem"),
        new("report_gap", "Posts what the assistant had to work around as a public GitHub discussion, after you agree"),
    ];

    private static readonly Tool[] BuildTools =
    [
        new("list_items", "Lists the starters, kits, pages and blocks, and which are Pro"),
        new("describe_item", "Lists an item's settings, the components it uses and its render mode support"),
        new("add_item", "Adds a block or a page to your app, at its own place or at a line"),
        new("add_crud", "Creates a page to display, add, edit and delete a model's records"),
        new("new_app", "Creates a new app from a starter or an app kit"),
        new("init_app", "Sets up IzzyBlazor in an app you already have"),
    ];

    private const string VsCodeJson = """
        {
          "servers": {
            "izzyblazor": {
              "type": "http",
              "url": "https://izzyblazor.com/mcp"
            }
          }
        }
        """;

    private const string CursorJson = """
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "izzyblazor": {
              "url": "https://izzyblazor.com/mcp"
            }
          }
        }
        """;
}
